{"name":"Alloy Orange","hex":"#c46210","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"alloy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A metal that is a combination of two or more elements, at least one of which is a metal, a base metal.","A metal of lesser value, mixed with a metal of greater value."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To mix or combine; often used of metals.","To reduce the purity of by mixing with a less valuable substance."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/alloy"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shades of orange","blocks":[{"text":"In optics, orange has a wavelength between approximately 585 and 620 nm and a hue of 30° in HSV color space. In the RGB color space it is a secondary color numerically halfway between gamma-compressed red and yellow, as can be seen in the RGB color wheel. The complementary color of orange is azure. Orange pigments are largely in the ochre or cadmium families, and absorb mostly blue light."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Shades_of_Orange.svg/330px-Shades_of_Orange.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_orange"},{"title":"Alloy","blocks":[{"text":"An alloy is a mixture of chemical elements of which in most cases at least one is a metallic element, although the word is also sometimes used for mixtures of elements; herein only metallic alloys are described. Metallic alloys often have properties that differ from those of the pure elements from which they are made. The vast majority of metals used for commercial purposes are alloyed to improve their properties or behavior, such as increased strength, hardness or corrosion resistance. Metals may also be alloyed to reduce their overall cost, for instance alloys of gold and copper."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Alloy_and_metal_samples_-_Beryllium-Copper%2C_Inconel%2C_Steel%2C_Titanium%2C_Aluminum%2C_Magnesium.jpg/330px-Alloy_and_metal_samples_-_Beryllium-Copper%2C_Inconel%2C_Steel%2C_Titanium%2C_Aluminum%2C_Magnesium.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alloy"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Sign_Road_Work_Ahead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Sign_Road_Work_Ahead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASign_Road_Work_Ahead.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Golden_Gate_Bridge%2C_North_view.jpg/500px-Golden_Gate_Bridge%2C_North_view.jpg?utm_source=en